/**
 * @defgroup drivers FlightOS Driver Components
 *
 * @brief Device-driver interfaces in kernel/ and their SPARC hardware
 *        realization in arch/.
 *
 * @page drivers_overview Driver Components Overview
 *
 * The driver code follows the same split used elsewhere in FlightOS. The
 * high-level interfaces and policy live in `include/` and `kernel/`; the
 * current hardware realization uses SPARC LEON register access, interrupt
 * numbering, cache operations, and platform address definitions from
 * `arch/sparc/`. The split is a source-level boundary. It is not evidence
 * that every component is portable to another target without a new backend.
 *
 * ## Components
 *
 * - @ref spi_driver provides the SPI message, device, and controller objects.
 *   @ref grspi_driver implements the current GRSPI controller; @ref ad7814_driver
 *   and @ref adc128s102_driver are SPI device clients.
 * - @ref grspw2 describes the GRSPW2 SpaceWire packet and routing driver.
 * - @ref noc_dma describes queued one- and two-dimensional NoC DMA transfers.
 * - @ref xentium_driver maps processing-network tasks to loaded Xentium ELF
 *   programs and exchanges commands through mailboxes.
 *
 * ## Architecture-specific implementation
 *
 * Driver register accesses use the SPARC LEON non-cached raw accessors in
 * `arch/sparc/include/asm/io.h`; the `ioread32be()` and `iowrite32be()` names
 * used by the drivers map to those accesses. `virt_to_phys()` maps to the
 * architecture physical-address conversion. IRQ registration and extended IRQ
 * numbering are supplied by `arch/sparc/include/irq.h`,
 * `arch/sparc/include/asm/irq.h`, and `arch/sparc/kernel/irq.c`. GRSPW2 and
 * Xentium also rely on target-specific fixed addresses and 32-bit DMA-visible
 * values. The exact portability requirements need review.
 *
 * ## High-level interfaces
 *
 * The generic driver-facing contracts are declared by `include/kernel/spictlr.h`,
 * `include/grspw2.h`, `include/noc_dma.h`, and the Xentium headers under
 * `include/kernel/`. Implementations are in `kernel/`; configuration gates are
 * in `kernel/Kconfig` and object selection is in `kernel/Makefile`.
 *
 * ## Component mapping
 *
 * | Component | High-level interface | Implementation and hardware boundary |
 * | --- | --- | --- |
 * | SPI | `include/kernel/spictlr.h` | `kernel/spictlr.c`, `kernel/grspi.c`, `arch/sparc/include/asm/io.h` |
 * | AD7814 | `kernel/ad7814_spi.c` | GRSPI plus caller-provided chip select; GR712 GPIO demo is conditional |
 * | ADC128S102 | `kernel/adc128s102_spi.c` | GRSPI plus caller-provided chip select; optional sysctl/demo support |
 * | GRSPW2 | `include/grspw2.h` | `kernel/grspw2.c`, LEON I/O/IRQ/cache helpers |
 * | NoC DMA | `include/noc_dma.h`, `include/noc.h` | `kernel/noc_dma.c`, MPPB/SSDP platform mappings, LEON I/O/IRQ helpers |
 * | Xentium | `include/kernel/xentium*.h` | `kernel/xentium.c`, NoC DMA, LEON I/O/IRQ helpers |
 *
 * `libc/src/grspw2.c` provides the user-facing syscall wrappers for GRSPW2;
 * syscall dispatch is in `kernel/syscalls.c`. Xentium ELF discovery is wired
 * through `init/modules-image.c`, while Xentium programs are built under
 * `dsp/xentium/`. The exact ownership of application-side setup remains
 * configuration-specific and needs review.
 *
 * ## Diagrams
 *
 * @plantumlfile drivers_architecture.puml
 * @plantumlfile spi_transfer_flow.puml
 * @plantumlfile grspw2_packet_activity.puml
 * @plantumlfile noc_dma_transfer_activity.puml
 * @plantumlfile xentium_task_activity.puml
 *
 * The diagrams show only relationships and control flow observed in the source.
 * Unimplemented asynchronous paths, incomplete platform support, and other
 * limitations are documented in the relevant component files and marked
 * "needs review" where their intended behavior is unclear.
 */
